From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 16:02:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: the built-in CA store answers with the certificate a
 title asks for

An empty store is not something a title walks past: Super Mario Party Jamboree asks for
NintendoCAG3 (CaCertificateId 1) as soon as it has a name for the NPLN front end, and an
empty answer is err::ShowError 0x270141 with not one nnsocketSocket in the trace - it gives
up before it opens a connection.

The store now holds one certificate for that id, in the layout the SDK walks: the entry, the
terminating entry (id All), then the DER data, every der_offset counted from the start of the
buffer, and the whole thing 4-byte aligned as the service does.

The DER is Nintendo's own certificate and we neither have it nor can ship it, so there are two
sources, in this order: <game data>/certs/1.der, which is where a user can drop what came out of
their console CertStore title (0100000000000800), and a stand-in generated here. The peer is
never validated against either - the handshake runs on SecureTransport, with verification
skipped for the redirected hosts - so the list is what makes a title start, not what decides
whether the connection is good.

Both spellings of the entry (BuiltInCertificateInfo and SDK 17 BuiltInCertificateInfoBase)
fill the store from the same code, and t_ssl_certs pins the layout, the terminator, the unknown
id, the file that wins and the offline failure.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: The console keeps its CA store in the CertStore system title (0100000000000800), which this host does not
 have and cannot ship, and an empty answer is not something a title walks past: Super Mario Party Jamboree asks
 for NintendoCAG3 (CaCertificateId 1) the moment it has a name for the NPLN front end, and reports
 err::ShowError 0x270141 without opening a single socket. The store now answers with one certificate, in the
 layout the SDK walks: the entry, the terminating entry (id All), then the DER data, every offset counted from
 the start of the buffer. The DER comes from <game data>/certs/1.der when the user dropped their own console's
 there, and from a built-in stand-in otherwise. Nothing validates a peer against this list - the handshake is
 SecureTransport's, with verification skipped for the redirected hosts - so the list is what lets a title start,
 not what decides whether the connection is good.
Issue: https://github.com/GigiFrack/NX2Apple (fork)

