+// certificate a title asks for by id.
+//
+// On the console the certificates live in the CertStore system title (0100000000000800). This host has
+// neither the title nor the right to ship it, and the peer it talks to is not the console's either: the
+// Nextendo servers answer every name with a self-signed certificate of their own (openssl s_client against
+// nex.nextendo.network says so), so no root in the world validates them. The chain a game collects here is
+// therefore never what decides the handshake - hle_ssl.c hands the connection to SecureTransport and skips
+// its verification for the redirected hosts.
+//
+// What the list does decide is whether a title starts at all. Super Mario Party Jamboree asks for
+// NintendoCAG3 (CaCertificateId 1) as soon as it has a name for the NPLN front end, and an empty answer
+// stops it before it opens a socket: err::ShowError 0x270141, and not one nnsocketSocket in the trace.
+//
+// The answer is an array of BuiltInCertificateInfo (0x18 bytes: id, status, der_size, der_offset) closed by
+// the terminator entry (id All = -1), with the DER data behind the table and every der_offset counted from
+// the start of the buffer.
+//
+// Two sources, in this order:
+//   <game data>/certs/<id>.der   a certificate the user dropped there (their own console's store, out of
+//                               0100000000000800) - it wins, so the real one can be used without a build
+//   this file                    a stand-in for NintendoCAG3, generated here and self-signed
+//
+// The stand-in carries Nintendo's subject name so that a title matching on the name still matches, but the
+// key is ours: nothing signs with it and nothing but this build trusts it.
+#pragma once
+
+#include <stdint.h>
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
